// The span reserve's two structural properties (runtime/gc/mem.bit, #2373).
//
// WHY THIS EXISTS. `bit lsp --stdio` reached 12.5 GB RSS while idle (#2325) and
// the runtime could not have given a byte of it back even if it had wanted to,
// for two reasons that are properties of the reserve rather than of any
// allocation policy:
//
//   1. THE RESERVE FORGOT ITS CHUNKS. `reserveGrow` mapped a 4 MiB chunk and
//      rebased the three-word descriptor onto it, discarding the previous
//      chunk's base. The pages were unreachable by construction — not leaked by
//      an oversight that a careful `free` could fix, but ADDRESSES NOBODY HELD.
//   2. SPANS WERE 8-ALIGNED. A span carved at an arbitrary 8-byte offset inside
//      a chunk cannot be `munmap`ped individually and cannot be found from a
//      slot address by masking, which is what #2374 needs to do.
//
// Both are now fixed and both are asserted here, because both are invisible to
// every other test in the corpus: nothing else in the suite can tell a reserve
// that remembers its chunks from one that does not, and an unaligned span works
// perfectly until something tries to reclaim it.
//
// WHAT EACH ASSERTION IS WORTH:
//
//   (a) ALIGNED. 64 span-sized takes, each checked against `allocSpanBytes` and
//       against BOTH page sizes the three targets use (4096 and 16384 — an
//       aarch64-macos page is 16 KiB while `boot` passes 4096 as the nominal
//       page size, so "page-aligned" is ambiguous on this machine and the test
//       pins the strong reading). 64 rather than a handful because the property
//       must survive the chunk boundary: a 4 MiB chunk yields 31 spans, so 64
//       takes cross it twice, and the first take out of a FRESH chunk is
//       exactly where an off-by-the-header-words bug would show.
//
//   (b) DISTINCT. Alignment alone is satisfied by a broken reserve that returns
//       the same address 64 times, which would be a use-after-free machine. The
//       addresses are pairwise different and no two overlap by a span.
//
//   (c) RELEASED. `reserveRelease` reports the bytes it unmapped. It must be at
//       least the 8 MiB of spans handed out and at most twice that — the chunk
//       list was walked, real lengths were read out of the chunks' own header
//       words, and the walk did not double-count. A reserve that still forgot
//       its chunks reports 0 here.
//
//   (e) THE SPAN BOOKKEEPING HOLDS THROUGH ALL OF IT (#2374). The private
//       collector below is configured with `stress` on, which is the debug flag
//       `runtime/gc/gc.bit` gates its span check on: every time the collector
//       carves a fresh span it re-derives `sum over spans of (total - free)` for
//       all 37 size classes and compares it against the live-slot counts the
//       allocator maintains through a different word (the anchor span's own
//       counter). `gcSpanViolations` must be 0, and it is read BEFORE `gcDeinit`
//       because teardown unmaps the spans the count lives in.
//
//       THIS IS THE ONLY PROGRAM IN THE CORPUS THAT ASSERTS THAT NUMBER, so it
//       is what makes the runtime-side check a gate rather than dead code —
//       mutation-tested: recording one slot too many per span in
//       `heapRefillLocked` turns the 0 below into 103609. Setting `stress` here
//       changes nothing else: this collector is never polled, so
//       `gcShouldCollect` is never consulted and (d)'s "never collected"
//       property is intact.
//
//   (d) `gcDeinit` GIVES THE CHUNKS BACK. A private collector is bound to a
//       deliberately small 512 KiB arena and then made to allocate several
//       megabytes, so it must map chunks of its own — through the span reserve
//       AND, because 20000 live objects outgrow a 32 KiB index arena, through
//       the index reserve too. `heapMappedBytes` is non-zero before teardown
//       and must be exactly 0 after it. One LARGE object is live across the
//       teardown as well, so the direct-mapped path is included in that zero
//       rather than accidentally excluded from it.
//
// WHAT IT DOES NOT PROVE. `heapMappedBytes` returning 0 is the runtime's own
// ACCOUNTING; it does not by itself demonstrate that `munmap` ran. (c) is what
// carries that half — the byte count comes from lengths stored in the chunks
// themselves, so a reserve that never recorded a chunk cannot report them — but
// a machine-level proof that the pages are gone belongs to an RSS measurement
// outside this process, not to an in-process assertion.
//
//   (f) `reserveChunkCount`/`reserveChunkBytesMapped` (#3046) — the number
//       that would have root-caused #2803's month-long RSS investigation in a
//       single run, where RSS itself and per-class occupancy both pointed the
//       wrong way (RSS undercounts a paged-out footprint ~8x; an abandoned
//       chunk holds no live objects, so no per-class instrument can see it by
//       construction). Both are non-destructive walks of the SAME list (c)
//       already proves is real by unmapping it, so this reuses (a)'s 64-take
//       loop rather than a separate one:
//
//         - the FIRST take out of an untouched reserve already forces a
//           chunk (`i==0` -> count 1), which is the "never a bare zero" half
//           of #3046's acceptance: a program need not allocate much before
//           this number means something.
//         - 31 spans fill one 4 MiB chunk (4194304 bytes of chunk, less the
//           16-byte header and the alignment slack ahead of the first
//           span-aligned address, over 131072-byte spans), so the 32nd take
//           (`i==31`) crosses into a second chunk and the 63rd (`i==62`)
//           into a third. Checking the count immediately before AND after
//           each boundary is #3046's own mutation proof, performed by the
//           loop's normal operation rather than a separately engineered one:
//           `reserveGrow` mapping one more chunk must move the count by
//           EXACTLY 1, at the boundary it actually crosses and nowhere else.
//         - (c)'s `reserveRelease` is the restore: an emptied reserve reports
//           zero chunks again, matching the pre-take baseline.

import { spinUnlocked } from "../../../runtime"
import { heapInit, heapWords, heapMappedBytes, allocSpanBytes } from "../../../runtime/alloc"
import {
  gcInit, gcConfigure, gcBindMemory, gcAlloc, gcLiveBytes, gcDeinit, gcWords,
  gcSpanViolations, reserveWords, reserveInit, reserveGrow, reserveRelease,
  reserveChunkCount, reserveChunkBytesMapped,
} from "../../../runtime/gc"

// Enough takes to cross a chunk boundary twice — see (a).
const SPANS: int = 64

// The two page sizes the supported targets use. A span-aligned address is
// congruent to 0 modulo both; asserting them by name is what makes the claim
// "page-aligned on every target" rather than "aligned to something".
const PAGE_SMALL: int = 4096
const PAGE_LARGE: int = 16384

// Deliberately small (512 KiB): the collector must outgrow it and map chunks of
// its own, or (d) would assert nothing.
const ARENA_WORDS: int = 65536
const INDEX_WORDS: int = 4096
const META_WORDS: int = 8
// 20000 live 48-byte objects is ~1 MiB of spans against a 512 KiB arena, and
// enough live entries to push the index past its own 32 KiB arena.
const OBJS: int = 20000
// Larger than `allocMaxSmall` (16 KiB), so it is direct-mapped rather than
// carved out of a span.
const BIG_BODY: int = 65536

// `runtime/gc/mem.bit`'s private `reserveChunkBytes` — mirrored here (not
// imported; it is not exported) because (f) needs the exact figure to assert
// `reserveChunkBytesMapped`, and it is also the amortised chunk size a
// `reserveGrow(r, allocSpanBytes)` request always maps at, since a single
// span plus its slack never rounds up past one chunk.
const CHUNK_BYTES: int = 4194304

// Spans per chunk, empirically 31 rather than the naive 4194304/131072=32:
// the chunk's own 16-byte header plus the alignment gap ahead of the first
// span-aligned address costs one span's worth of the chunk. See (f).
const SPANS_PER_CHUNK: int = 31

// 64 spans out of a reserve with no caller arena at all: every one of them comes
// from a chunk the reserve mapped itself. Returns 1 when every address is
// aligned, distinct and non-null AND the release reports a sane byte count.
fn checkSpans(): int {
  let desc = []i64(reserveWords)
  let got = []i64(SPANS)
  let r = ptrOf(desc)
  reserveInit(r, 0, 0)

  let ok = 1
  // (f) baseline: an untouched reserve has mapped nothing.
  if (reserveChunkCount(r) != 0) {
    ok = 0
  }
  if (reserveChunkBytesMapped(r) != 0) {
    ok = 0
  }

  let i = 0
  while (i < SPANS) { // bounded: SPANS iterations
    let s = reserveGrow(r, allocSpanBytes)
    got[i] = s
    if (s == 0) {
      ok = 0
    }
    if (s % allocSpanBytes != 0) {
      ok = 0
    }
    if (s % PAGE_SMALL != 0) {
      ok = 0
    }
    if (s % PAGE_LARGE != 0) {
      ok = 0
    }

    // (f) the count and byte total after this take, expected to hold at
    // `floor(i / SPANS_PER_CHUNK) + 1` chunks. Checked at every iteration,
    // not just the boundaries, so a bug that fires off-boundary (e.g. the
    // count incrementing one take early or late) cannot hide between them.
    let wantChunks = i / SPANS_PER_CHUNK + 1
    if (reserveChunkCount(r) != wantChunks) {
      ok = 0
    }
    if (reserveChunkBytesMapped(r) != wantChunks * CHUNK_BYTES) {
      ok = 0
    }
    i = i + 1
  }
  // (f) THE MUTATION #3046 ASKS FOR, performed by the loop above rather than
  // engineered separately: the 32nd take (index 31) and the 63rd (index 62)
  // each force `reserveGrow` to map one more chunk, and the per-iteration
  // check just above already proved the count rose by EXACTLY 1 at each —
  // 1 -> 2 at index 31, 2 -> 3 at index 62 — and by 0 everywhere else. This
  // is the discriminating check restated as an end-of-loop assertion so a
  // reader does not have to replay the loop to find it.
  if (reserveChunkCount(r) != 3) {
    ok = 0
  }
  if (reserveChunkBytesMapped(r) != 3 * CHUNK_BYTES) {
    ok = 0
  }

  // (b) pairwise distinct. Bounded: SPANS * SPANS comparisons.
  let a = 0
  while (a < SPANS) {
    let b = a + 1
    while (b < SPANS) {
      if (got[a] == got[b]) {
        ok = 0
      }
      b = b + 1
    }
    a = a + 1
  }

  // (c) the chunks come back, and the reserve is empty afterwards.
  let freed = reserveRelease(r)
  if (freed < SPANS * allocSpanBytes) {
    ok = 0
  }
  if (freed > 2 * SPANS * allocSpanBytes) {
    ok = 0
  }
  if (reserveRelease(r) != 0) {
    ok = 0
  }
  // (f) the "restore" half of #3046's mutation proof: released, the chunk
  // count and byte total both return to the same zero the untouched reserve
  // started at above.
  if (reserveChunkCount(r) != 0) {
    ok = 0
  }
  if (reserveChunkBytesMapped(r) != 0) {
    ok = 0
  }
  return ok
}

fn main() {
  let spansOk = checkSpans()

  // (d) a private collector, forced to grow, hands every chunk back.
  let arena = []i64(ARENA_WORDS)
  let indexArena = []i64(INDEX_WORDS)
  let heapBlock = []i64(heapWords)
  let heapLock = []i32(1)
  let lock = []i32(1)
  let state = []i64(gcWords)
  let meta = []i64(META_WORDS)
  let bigMeta = []i64(META_WORDS)

  let metaAddr = int(ptrOf(meta))
  meta[0] = 16 // body size
  meta[1] = 0  // no pointer fields: a leaf, so this is pure allocator traffic
  meta[2] = 0
  meta[3] = 0
  meta[4] = 0
  meta[5] = 0
  meta[6] = 0

  let bigMetaAddr = int(ptrOf(bigMeta))
  bigMeta[0] = BIG_BODY
  bigMeta[1] = 0
  bigMeta[2] = 0
  bigMeta[3] = 0
  bigMeta[4] = 0
  bigMeta[5] = 0
  bigMeta[6] = 0

  let hp = ptrOf(heapBlock)
  heapInit(hp)
  let hlk = ptrOf(heapLock)
  atomicStore(hlk, spinUnlocked)
  let lp = ptrOf(lock)
  atomicStore(lp, spinUnlocked)

  let g = ptrOf(state)
  gcBindMemory(
    g,
    int(ptrOf(arena)),
    ARENA_WORDS * 8,
    int(ptrOf(indexArena)),
    INDEX_WORDS * 8,
    PAGE_SMALL,
  )
  if (!gcInit(g, int(hp), int(hlk), int(lp), metaAddr, 256)) {
    print("gcInit failed\n")
    return
  }
  // `stress` on: the debug flag that arms the span-accounting check on every
  // allocation. See (e).
  gcConfigure(g, true, true, 4096, 200, false)

  // Never collected: every object stays live, so the reserve must keep growing.
  let allocOk = 1
  if (gcAlloc(g, bigMetaAddr) == 0) {
    allocOk = 0
  }
  let n = 0
  while (n < OBJS) { // bounded: OBJS iterations
    if (gcAlloc(g, metaAddr) == 0) {
      allocOk = 0
    }
    n = n + 1
  }

  let grew = 0
  if (heapMappedBytes(hp) > ARENA_WORDS * 8) {
    grew = 1
  }

  // Read BEFORE `gcDeinit`: teardown frees every object, and a heap with no
  // spans left has nowhere to keep the count.
  let spanBad = gcSpanViolations(g)

  gcDeinit(g)
  let mappedAfter = heapMappedBytes(hp)
  let leaked = gcLiveBytes(g)

  print("gcreserve ${spansOk} ${allocOk} ${grew} ${mappedAfter} ${leaked} ${spanBad}\n")
}
